From: mayipashu <mayipashu@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 25 Nov 2026 22:10:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] net: airoha: npu: wait for firmware bring-up before the
 version query

The ClankerNPU firmware brings itself up from inside the RISC-V image:
it pulses the SCU reset, initialises PLIC, its own console and the timer,
then waits for every hart to sync before it answers the mailbox. On a
cold boot that takes noticeably longer than the 100 ms the driver sleeps
after releasing the harts plus the 100 ms mailbox timeout, so the
one-shot version query times out and is never retried. The NPU looks
dead even though it finished initialising.

Observed on H3C HM2004-DU (AN7581 + MT7916): the host console showed no
"NPU fw version" line at all, while MIB31 read back 0xCCCCCCCC
(INIT_COMPLETE), i.e. the firmware was up and healthy.

Poll MIB31 for the completion signature before querying, then retry the
query a few times. Both steps are limited to boards that pin an image
through "firmware-name", so the generic image keeps its old timing. The
signature is ClankerNPU specific, so a poll timeout is not fatal, and a
final warning replaces the silent failure.

Signed-off-by: mayipashu <mayipashu@users.noreply.github.com>
---
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_npu.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_npu.c
@@ -46,6 +46,16 @@
 #define REG_CR_MBQ8_CTRL(_n)		(NPU_MBOX_BASE_ADDR + 0x0b0 + ((_n) << 2))
 #define REG_CR_NPU_MIB(_n)		(NPU_MBOX_BASE_ADDR + 0x140 + ((_n) << 2))
 
+/*
+ * ClankerNPU writes this signature to MIB31 once its bring-up finished.
+ * MIB31 is the last register of the NPU parameter block.
+ */
+#define NPU_BOOT_SIGNATURE_REG		REG_CR_NPU_MIB(31)
+#define NPU_BOOT_SIGNATURE_COMPLETE	0xcccccccc
+#define NPU_BOOT_POLL_RETRIES		20	/* 20 * 100 ms = 2 s */
+#define NPU_BOOT_POLL_INTERVAL_MS	100
+#define NPU_VERSION_RETRIES		5	/* 5 * 100 ms = 500 ms */
+
 #define NPU_WLAN_BASE_ADDR		0x30d000
 
 #define REG_IRQ_STATUS			(NPU_WLAN_BASE_ADDR + 0x030)
@@ -797,10 +807,44 @@
 	regmap_write(npu->regmap, REG_CR_BOOT_TRIGGER, 0x1);
 	msleep(100);
 
-	if (!airoha_npu_wlan_msg_get(npu, 0, WLAN_FUNC_GET_WAIT_NPU_VERSION,
-				     &val, sizeof(val), GFP_KERNEL))
-		dev_info(dev, "NPU fw version: %0d.%d\n",
-			 (val >> 16) & 0xffff, val & 0xffff);
+	/*
+	 * The firmware needs a moment to bring itself up: it pulses the SCU
+	 * reset, initialises PLIC, its console and the timer, and waits for
+	 * every hart to sync. A bare 100 ms mailbox timeout is not enough on
+	 * a cold boot, and a single failed query is not retried, so the
+	 * version was lost and the NPU looked dead even though it had
+	 * finished initialising (MIB31 == 0xCCCCCCCC).
+	 *
+	 * Poll the firmware's completion signature first, then query the
+	 * version with a few retries. The signature is ClankerNPU specific,
+	 * so a timeout is not fatal: firmware that never writes it still
+	 * gets its query attempts.
+	 */
+	if (of_find_property(dev->of_node, "firmware-name", NULL)) {
+		for (i = 0; i < NPU_BOOT_POLL_RETRIES; i++) {
+			u32 sig = 0;
+
+			if (!regmap_read(npu->regmap,
+					 NPU_BOOT_SIGNATURE_REG, &sig) &&
+			    sig == NPU_BOOT_SIGNATURE_COMPLETE)
+				break;
+			msleep(NPU_BOOT_POLL_INTERVAL_MS);
+		}
+	}
+
+	for (i = 0; i < NPU_VERSION_RETRIES; i++) {
+		if (!airoha_npu_wlan_msg_get(npu, 0,
+					     WLAN_FUNC_GET_WAIT_NPU_VERSION,
+					     &val, sizeof(val), GFP_KERNEL)) {
+			dev_info(dev, "NPU fw version: %0d.%d\n",
+				 (val >> 16) & 0xffff, val & 0xffff);
+			break;
+		}
+		msleep(NPU_BOOT_POLL_INTERVAL_MS);
+	}
+
+	if (i == NPU_VERSION_RETRIES)
+		dev_warn(dev, "NPU firmware did not answer the version query\n");
 
 	platform_set_drvdata(pdev, npu);
 
